<p>
The much predicted succession of Apple&rsquo;s top management is underway. In a <a href="https://www.apple.com/community-letter-from-tim/">community letter from Tim Cook</a>, Cook announced that he is transitioning to Apple&rsquo;s executive chairman and as long expected, John Ternus, the current Senior Vice President of Engineering, will succeed him as CEO.
</p>
<p>
Although I am a long term, happy resident of Apple&rsquo;s walled garden, I&rsquo;m not really one of the Fan Boyz so I can look at Apple relatively dispassionately. I <i>do</i> think their hardware is better than the competition&rsquo;s but the thing that keeps me in the Apple ecosphere is their privacy story and a perceived concern for their users. I&rsquo;m not completely naive about that concern but at the very least it&rsquo;s better than what you get from Google and the Android vendors.
</p>
<p>
There&rsquo;s plenty not to like though. The worst of those things is Apple&rsquo;s embracing of advertising. Much of their privacy success and user-friendliness comes from their <i>not</i> being an advertising-based company. For companies like Apple, ads are a corrupting influence that is antithetical to privacy and that ultimately replaces the user with the advertiser as the customer. When you think about, many of Apple&rsquo;s perception problems are rooted in advertising.
</p>
<p>
By all accounts, Ternus <i>is</i> concerned about Apple&rsquo;s customers and cares about Apple&rsquo;s &ldquo;insanely great&rdquo; reputation. Perhaps he&rsquo;ll nudge Apple away from advertising and back to the customer.</p>
